From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Subject: v8: ppc64 drop the redundant extsw from ChangeInt32ToInt64

VisitChangeInt32ToInt64 still carries mbrandy's original
"TODO: inspect input to see if nop is appropriate" and emits
kPPC_ExtendSignWord32 unconditionally.  Two of its input shapes already hold
the sign extension the extsw would write.

    sar      srawi r4,r4,1          ChangeInt32ToInt64(Word32Sar(x, n))
             extsw r3,r4            -> the extsw is the identity

    extsb    extsb r4,r3            ChangeInt32ToInt64(Word32SignExtend8(x))
             extsw r3,r4            -> the extsw is the identity

    trunc    extsw r4,r3            ChangeInt32ToInt64(TruncateWord64ToWord32(x))
             extsw r3,r4            -> extsw(extsw(x)); one suffices

The first two become a rename and the third becomes a single extsw against the
truncate's own input.

Not a general claim about Word32 values.  Word32 is NOT canonically
sign-extended in this backend: kPPC_LoadWordU32 is lwz, so a negative int32
read from memory sits zero-extended, and the withdrawn
ppc64le-v8-int32-overflow-cmpd.patch shipped a miscompile by assuming
otherwise.  This is a whitelist of two producers, both guaranteed by the Power
ISA and not by any V8 convention:

  * VisitWord32Sar emits kPPC_ShiftRightAlg32, i.e. sraw or srawi.  Book I,
    "Shift Right Algebraic Word": the result is
    r&m | EXTS(RS[32])&~m -- a 64-bit value that is the sign extension of its
    own low word for every shift amount, and it reads only RS[32:63], so the
    input's upper word cannot reach it.  VisitWord32Sar also matches the
    shl+sar 16 and shl+sar 24 idioms and emits kPPC_ExtendSignWord16 /
    kPPC_ExtendSignWord8 instead; extsh and extsb sign-extend to 64 bits too,
    so either selection is covered.
  * VisitSignExtendWord8ToInt32 / VisitSignExtendWord16ToInt32 emit
    kPPC_ExtendSignWord8 / kPPC_ExtendSignWord16, i.e. extsb / extsh.

The truncate case needs no whitelist entry at all.  kPPC_Int64ToInt32 happens
to be an extsw today, but rather than rest on that, the patch emits one extsw
against the truncate's input directly -- extsw of x is by definition the sign
extension of x's low word, whatever the truncate would have expanded to -- and
covers the truncate.  Machine lowering builds that shape for every lossless
int64 -> int32 check, Word64Equal(ChangeInt32ToInt64(TruncateWord64ToWord32(v)),
v), at machine-lowering-reducer-inl.h:90, :997 and :1211, which is what guards
BigInt64 element stores and the Smi range checks; it is also what
i64.extend32_s compiles to.

kPPC_Add32, kPPC_Sub32, kPPC_Neg32 and kPPC_Mul32 are deliberately NOT
whitelisted even though all four end in an extsw in this tree
(code-generator-ppc.cc, the "if (instr->arch_opcode() == kPPC_Add32)" tails and
the two mul32-neg32-overflow-cmpd sequences), so eliding on top of them would
be correct here.  That is a V8 canonicalisation and not an ISA property, and it
is demonstrably not stable: node 26.4.0's V8 14.6 on this machine compiles
i64.extend_i32_s(i32.mul(x,3)) to "mullw r4,r4,r5; extsw r3,r4" and
i64.extend_i32_s(i32.sub(0,x)) to "neg r4,r4; extsw r3,r4" -- one extsw, not
two -- while compiling i64.extend_i32_s(i32.add(x,7)) to
"addi r4,r4,7; extsw r4,r4; extsw r3,r4".  A whitelist entry that silently
turns into a miscompile when a code-generator tail moves is not worth one
instruction, so those four are left alone.  If the Word32 canonicalisation is
ever removed outright -- which is the larger change this one deliberately is
not -- nothing here has to be revisited.

Also absent, and not elidable: the logical ops (kPPC_And/Or/Xor are the 64-bit
instructions, so an upper word survives into the result), shl, shr and the
rotates (slw, srw and rlwinm zero-extend), kPPC_Div32 and kPPC_Mod32 (bare
divw/modsw, whose RT[0:31] the ISA leaves undefined), kPPC_Uint32ToUint64
(clrldi), the loads, and anything whose value does not come from a single
instruction, such as a Phi.  kPPC_Cntlz32 (cntlzw, result 0..32) is elidable
and left out only because widening a clz to int64 is rare.

Why this is safe for the three consumers that can read the upper 32 bits of a
Word32 value.  Every case here is the IDENTITY on the 64-bit register: the bits
are the same before and after, so no consumer can tell the difference.  That is
the whole correctness argument, and it is why the patch is this narrow.  The
audit behind it, which is worth recording because it was the reason this was
deferred:

  * The deoptimizer never reads the upper word of a Word32.
    code-generator.cc:1476 and :1523 emit StoreInt32StackSlot /
    StoreInt32Register for MachineType::Int32(); translated-state.cc:1429 reads
    a register as static_cast<int32_t>(intptr_t) and :1631 reads a slot with
    GetUInt32Slot, which is Memory<uint32_t>(address) on little-endian.  Both
    take exactly 32 bits.  Maglev reaches the same two translation opcodes
    (maglev-code-generator.cc:1491, :1521) and therefore the same reads.
  * The ELFv2 argument convention is untouched because the register contents
    are untouched.  Worth recording separately: the ppc64 backend does not
    extend Word32 C-call arguments at all.  kArchCallCFunction
    (code-generator-ppc.cc:1101) places them through AssembleMove, which is
    Move/mr for a register and StoreU64 for a stack slot, with no extsw
    anywhere, while liftoff does extend them
    (liftoff-assembler-ppc-inl.h:3176).  Since lwz leaves a negative int32
    zero-extended, V8/ppc64 can already hand a C function an int argument whose
    high word is not the sign extension the ABI asks for.  Stack arguments are
    safe on little-endian -- the callee reads the low four bytes of the
    doubleword -- so the exposure is register arguments to callees that rely on
    PROMOTE_MODE.  That is pre-existing, is not made worse or better here, and
    deserves its own look.
  * Maglev is not touched: this is the TurboFan instruction selector, and
    Maglev has its own code generator (maglev-ir-ppc.cc, which extsw's every
    Int32 result itself).  The two share only the deopt translation above.

One mechanical point.  The rename makes a register defined with
MachineRepresentation::kWord32 be read as kWord64.  That already happens in
this file -- VisitBitcastWord32ToWord64, twenty lines above, is an
EmitIdentity across exactly that boundary and is live in a pointer-compression
build -- and it is safe here because ByteWidthForStackSlot
(register-allocation.h:71) gives kWord32 kSystemPointerSize, the same 8 bytes
as kWord64, and this backend's AssembleMove stores and loads integer stack
slots with StoreU64/LoadU64 regardless of representation.

Verified on POWER9 (AC922, DD2.3) by executing the instructions: 8087 checks,
0 failures.  extsw is idempotent; srawi for every shift 0..31 and sraw for
every register shift amount 0..63 (including ones with garbage above bit 5)
leave a value equal to its own extsw, ignore the input's upper word, and agree
with C's int32_t >> for shifts below 32; extsb and extsh likewise.  The
exclusions were measured rather than assumed: over 400 operand pairs, slw left
a non-sign-extended result 199 times, srw 84, and 197, or 279, xor 316, add
338, subf 311, mullw 233, rlwinm 9, and divw 75 -- divw(-7, 2) returns
0x00000000fffffffd on this hardware, so kPPC_Div32's result really is not
sign-extended.  The premise was re-checked in the same run: lwz of 0x80000000
gives 0x0000000080000000, lwa gives 0xffffffff80000000.

Measured opportunity, from TurboFan disassembly of hand-built Wasm on this
machine (node --no-liftoff --print-wasm-code; V8 14.6, so indicative of the
shapes rather than of 153's exact output): i64.extend_i32_s(i32.shr_s(load,1))
compiles to "lwzx; srawi; extsw", i64.extend_i32_s(i32.extend8_s(x)) to
"extsb; extsw", i64.extend_i32_s(i32.shr_s(a,b)) to "sraw; extsw", and
i64.extend_i32_s(i32.wrap_i64(x)) to "extsw; extsw" -- one instruction saved in
each.  The negative controls kept theirs: i64.extend_i32_s(i32.load) and the
and/or/xor/shl/shr/div forms all stayed "lwzx; extsw".  On the JS side the
shape that matters is ChangeInt32ToInt64(UntagSmi(x)): with 31-bit Smis UntagSmi
is Word32ShiftRightArithmeticShiftOutZeros (assembler.h:2811), so every
Smi-to-intptr conversion through
ConvertJSPrimitiveToUntagged/UntaggedKind::kInt64
(machine-lowering-reducer-inl.h:1278) is srawi followed by a dead extsw.

NOT COMPILED AND NOT BENCHMARKED.  No chromium build was made for this patch.
The inserted code was syntax-checked in isolation against a stub mirror of the
Turboshaft API, and every name it uses (Opcode::kWordUnary, Opcode::kShift,
WordUnaryOp::Kind::kSignExtend8/16, ShiftOp::Kind::kShiftRightArithmetic and
kShiftRightArithmeticShiftOutZeros, ShiftOp::rep, WordUnaryOp::rep,
Opmask::kTruncateWord64ToWord32, ChangeOp::input) was read out of this tree's
operations.h, opmasks.h and the surrounding file, but the real translation unit
has not been through a compiler.  The figures above are instruction counts and
disassembly, not timings.

Correctness battery: /var/tmp/v8set-test/test.js grew 1388 checks for this,
1579 in total, all passing under node 26.4.0 -- in the default configuration,
under --no-liftoff (every Wasm function through TurboFan) and under
--liftoff --no-wasm-tier-up (Liftoff only, which always emits a plain extsw for
i64_signextend_i32 and so acts as the reference tier).  The Wasm half builds a
module by hand so that each i64.extend_i32_s has a chosen producer, reads every
case once before warming and again after 40000 calls, and requires the two
tiers to agree with each other and with the JS oracle.  The inputs are the ones
that catch this class of bug: values read out of linear memory with lwz so that
bit 31 is set and the upper word is zero, INT32_MIN and INT32_MAX, i64 inputs
whose high word is garbage (0xdeadbeef80000000 and friends) for the truncate
shape, and the non-whitelisted producers as negative controls -- with mem[0] =
-1, dropping the extsw in the and/or/xor/shl/shr/div forms would return
4294967295 instead of -1, which is what those cases assert against.  The JS
half drives Int32Array -> BigInt64Array round trips, shifted and arithmetic
widenings, BigInt64Array -> Number (the lossless-int64 check), and Smi ->
BigInt across the Smi range.

Valid for both pools.  extsw, sraw, srawi, extsb and extsh are base Book I
instructions, absent from the ISA 3.0C Book III table of instructions
controlled by the V2.07 bit; the patch adds no instruction at all, it removes
them.

No ordering dependency on the rest of the series.  It touches only
VisitChangeInt32ToInt64 and inserts a helper above it;
ppc64le-v8-tst32-dead-extsw.patch is the same idea in the code generator for
kPPC_Tst32 and does not overlap, and the VisitChangeUint32ToUint64 peephole
that ppc64le-v8-select-roundtieseven-minmax.patch adds forty lines below --
which drops an extsw whose bits a following clrldi overwrites -- is the
complement of this one from the consumer side.  Upstreamable as-is; not
ppc64le packaging specific.  arm64 (kArm64Sbfx on the sar shape) and riscv64
(IsSignExtendWord32ToWord64 -> kArchNop) already do the equivalent.

--- a/v8/src/compiler/backend/ppc/instruction-selector-ppc.cc
+++ b/v8/src/compiler/backend/ppc/instruction-selector-ppc.cc
@@ -1336,8 +1336,89 @@
   EmitIdentity(node);
 }
 
+namespace {
+
+// Does the instruction this backend selects for {op} leave a 64-bit register
+// whose bits 32..63 already hold the sign extension of bit 31?  Where that
+// holds, the kPPC_ExtendSignWord32 on top of it writes the bits that are
+// already there and can be dropped.
+//
+// Word32 values are not canonically sign-extended in this backend --
+// kPPC_LoadWordU32 is lwz, so a negative int32 read from memory sits
+// zero-extended -- so this is a whitelist of individual producers and never a
+// property of Word32 values in general.  Both entries are guaranteed by the
+// Power ISA alone and not by any V8 convention:
+//
+//   Word32 sar   VisitWord32Sar emits kPPC_ShiftRightAlg32 (sraw/srawi, whose
+//                64-bit result the ISA defines as EXTS of the shifted word),
+//                or, for the shl+sar 16/24 idioms, kPPC_ExtendSignWord16 and
+//                kPPC_ExtendSignWord8 (extsh/extsb).  All three sign-extend.
+//   Word32 SignExtend8/16   kPPC_ExtendSignWord8/16 -> extsb/extsh.
+//
+// kPPC_Add32, kPPC_Sub32, kPPC_Neg32 and kPPC_Mul32 do end in an extsw today
+// and would be elidable too, but that is a V8 canonicalisation rather than an
+// ISA guarantee -- V8 14.6 emits kPPC_Mul32 and kPPC_Neg32 without it -- so
+// they are deliberately left out rather than coupling this to code that may
+// move.  Also absent, and not elidable at all: the logical ops (and/or/xor are
+// 64-bit), shl, shr and the rotates (slw/srw/rlwinm zero-extend), kPPC_Div32
+// and kPPC_Mod32 (bare divw/modsw, whose high word the ISA leaves undefined
+// and which measure non-sign-extended on POWER9), kPPC_Uint32ToUint64
+// (clrldi), the loads, and anything whose value does not come from one
+// instruction, such as a Phi.
+bool Word32ResultIsSignExtended(const Operation& op) {
+  switch (op.opcode) {
+    case Opcode::kWordUnary: {
+      const WordUnaryOp& unop = op.Cast<WordUnaryOp>();
+      return unop.rep == WordRepresentation::Word32() &&
+             (unop.kind == WordUnaryOp::Kind::kSignExtend8 ||
+              unop.kind == WordUnaryOp::Kind::kSignExtend16);
+    }
+    case Opcode::kShift: {
+      const ShiftOp& shift = op.Cast<ShiftOp>();
+      return shift.rep == WordRepresentation::Word32() &&
+             (shift.kind == ShiftOp::Kind::kShiftRightArithmetic ||
+              shift.kind == ShiftOp::Kind::kShiftRightArithmeticShiftOutZeros);
+    }
+    default:
+      return false;
+  }
+}
+
+}  // namespace
+
 void InstructionSelector::VisitChangeInt32ToInt64(OpIndex node) {
-  // TODO(mbrandy): inspect input to see if nop is appropriate.
+  // TODO(mbrandy): inspect input to see if nop is appropriate.  Done below for
+  // the two producers the ISA already leaves sign-extended, and for the
+  // Word64 truncate; everything else still needs the extsw.
+  const ChangeOp& change = Cast<ChangeOp>(node);
+  const Operation& input_op = this->Get(change.input());
+
+  // ChangeInt32ToInt64(TruncateWord64ToWord32(x)) is extsw(extsw(x)): the
+  // truncate is kPPC_Int64ToInt32, which is an extsw of its own.  Emit a single
+  // extsw against x rather than dropping the second one, so that the result
+  // does not depend on what kPPC_Int64ToInt32 expands to.  Machine lowering
+  // builds this shape for every lossless int64 -> int32 check (the
+  // Word64Equal(ChangeInt32ToInt64(TruncateWord64ToWord32(v)), v) guarding
+  // BigInt64 and Smi stores), and it is also what i64.extend32_s compiles to.
+  if (input_op.Is<Opmask::kTruncateWord64ToWord32>() &&
+      CanCover(node, change.input())) {
+    PPCOperandGenerator g(this);
+    Emit(kPPC_ExtendSignWord32, g.DefineAsRegister(node),
+         g.UseRegister(input_op.Cast<ChangeOp>().input()));
+    return;
+  }
+
+  if (Word32ResultIsSignExtended(input_op)) {
+    // Rename instead of emitting.  The 64-bit register contents are bit for
+    // bit what the extsw would have produced, which is what leaves this
+    // value's frame-state, deoptimizer and C-call ABI consumers untouched.
+    // The same Word32-defined-register-used-as-Word64 rename already happens
+    // in VisitBitcastWord32ToWord64 above, and a Word32 spill slot is
+    // kSystemPointerSize wide here, the same as a Word64 one.
+    EmitIdentity(node);
+    return;
+  }
+
   VisitRR(this, kPPC_ExtendSignWord32, node);
 }
 
